### Identity RBAC REST Client
# Start Identity with `make identity` (or `go run ./scripts/start-local -service identity-service`) and Kong on port 8000.
# The first administrator must be bootstrapped with the SQL in RBAC.md.

@api = http://localhost:8000/api/v1
@adminEmail = admin@example.com
@adminPassword = AdminPassword123!
@customerEmail = rbac-customer@example.test
@customerPassword = SecurePassword123!
@adminToken = paste-admin-access-token-here
@customerToken = paste-customer-access-token-here
@adminUserId = 00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000
@customerUserId = 00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000

### Register administrator
# Copy data.userId into @adminUserId, then execute the SUPER_ADMIN bootstrap SQL.
# Re-running with the same email returns 409 EMAIL_ALREADY_REGISTERED.
# @name registerAdmin
POST {{api}}/auth/register
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "{{adminEmail}}",
  "password": "{{adminPassword}}",
  "fullName": "RBAC Administrator"
}


### Login administrator
# @name loginAdmin
POST {{api}}/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "admin@example.com",
  "password": "{{adminPassword}}"
}


### Register customer
# Registration assigns the CUSTOMER role automatically.
# @name registerCustomer
POST {{api}}/auth/register
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "{{customerEmail}}",
  "password": "{{customerPassword}}",
  "fullName": "RBAC Customer"
}


### Login customer
# @name loginCustomer
POST {{api}}/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "{{customerEmail}}",
  "password": "{{customerPassword}}"
}


